{"title":"2025 AMC 10A Problems/Problem 6","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Diagram\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Diagram</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_1\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_1.5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1.5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_3_(cheese)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3 (cheese)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_4_[SIMPLE:_ONLY_ISOSCELES_TRIANGLES]\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4 [SIMPLE: ONLY ISOSCELES TRIANGLES]</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_5_[Most_outer_triangle]\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5 [Most outer triangle]</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:00)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:00)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution(Quick_and_Easy)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution(Quick and Easy)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-12\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_DDM\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">12</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by DDM</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-13\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">13</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-14\"><a href=\"#Chinese_Video_Solution\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">14</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Chinese Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-15\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_SpreadTheMathLove\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">15</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by SpreadTheMathLove</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-16\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_(Done_in_1_Min)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">16</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution (Done in 1 Min)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-17\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">17</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-18\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_Thinking_Feet\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">18</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by Thinking Feet</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-19\"><a href=\"#Video_Solution_by_The_Beauty_of_Math\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">19</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Video Solution by The Beauty of Math</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-20\"><a href=\"#See_Also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">20</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See Also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem\">Problem</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>In an equilateral triangle each interior angle is trisected by a pair of rays. The intersection of the interiors of the middle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/7/0/3708b370b5211a4fa877395e33d6c67720f1dcbd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^{\\circ}$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />-angle at each vertex is the interior of a convex hexagon. What is the degree measure of the smallest angle of this hexagon?\n</p><p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/1/2/e1294885fcb9408c4eaf135aeb04adba289f49e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\textbf{(A) } 80 \\qquad\\textbf{(B) } 90 \\qquad\\textbf{(C) } 100 \\qquad\\textbf{(D) } 110 \\qquad\\textbf{(E) } 120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"428\" height=\"18\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Diagram\">Diagram</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Diagram\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/8/7/b8721c02782402bebcfd8a209cd82664eac73db8.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"[asy] /* AMC 10A 2025 Problem — Equilateral Triangle Trisectors Diagram */  import olympiad; size(220);  pair A = (0,0); pair B = (10,0); pair C = (5,8.660254037844385); // height = 5*sqrt(3)  // Triangle draw(A--B--C--cycle, linewidth(1));  // Mark vertices dot(&quot;$A$&quot;, A, SW); dot(&quot;$B$&quot;, B, SE); dot(&quot;$C$&quot;, C, N);  // Length of trisectors (visual only) real L = 8.8;  // Trisectors from each vertex (20° and 40° offsets from sides) draw(A--(A + L*dir(20)), blue+linewidth(0.8)); draw(A--(A + L*dir(40)), blue+linewidth(0.8));  draw(B--(B + L*dir(140)), blue+linewidth(0.8)); draw(B--(B + L*dir(160)), blue+linewidth(0.8));  draw(C--(C + L*dir(260)), blue+linewidth(0.8)); draw(C--(C + L*dir(280)), blue+linewidth(0.8));  // Intersection points (precomputed numerically) pair P1 = (4.07604, 3.4202); pair P2 = (5, 4.1955); pair P3 = (5.92396, 3.4202); pair P4 = (6.13341, 2.23238); pair P5 = (5, 1.81985); pair P6 = (3.86659, 2.23238);  // Hexagon interior filldraw(P1--P2--P3--P4--P5--P6--cycle, lightred+opacity(0.4), red+linewidth(0.8));  // Mark intersection region label(&quot;$P_1$&quot;, P1, NW); label(&quot;$P_2$&quot;, P2, N); label(&quot;$P_3$&quot;, P3, NE); label(&quot;$P_4$&quot;, P4, SE); label(&quot;$P_5$&quot;, P5, S); label(&quot;$P_6$&quot;, P6, SW);  label(&quot;Hexagon formed by the middle 20° trisectors&quot;, (5, -1.2)); [/asy]\" width=\"388\" height=\"368\" />\n</p><p>~Avs2010\n</p><p>~i_am_not_suk_at_math\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1\">Solution 1</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Assume you have a diagram in front of you.\n</p><p>Because each angle of the triangle is trisected, we have nine <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/9/9498d610c387e904044dd016ade3bc445cb5d100.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> angles.\nUsing a side of the triangle as a base, we have an isosceles triangle with two <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/9/9498d610c387e904044dd016ade3bc445cb5d100.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> angles. Using this we can show that the third angle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/8/4886119534bab6d6981008596733884b1a9e42c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$140^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. \n</p><p>Following that, we use the principle of vertical angles to show that one angle of the hexagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/8/4886119534bab6d6981008596733884b1a9e42c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$140^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />. And with rotational symmetry, three.\nThe average of all <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> angles has to be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/f/f3fd424e953ac76b50bf6103875acc049d21d3c2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$120^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/7/797c1d2e5cb34832c0ae44c52d209acf013bc847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" />\n- SpectralScholardih\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1.5\">Solution 1.5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1.5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Note that the hexagon’s interior angle sum is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/d/9/2d90a8212646e076fae82808b7469d47c369a370.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$720$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />, and due to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />-way symmetry there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> wider(bigger) angles and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> smaller ones. We can split these angles into <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> groups, each with one big angle and one small angle that sum up to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/c/fccb9a3e389e5cd6c5a351be5c83599385b59d4a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$720/3 = 240$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"99\" height=\"20\" /> degrees. Seeing this, we can eliminate answer choice <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/c/e/2ce997daa48c56bb3ad6be10110bfa193ce2d9f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(E)120$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"58\" height=\"20\" /> as it is asking for the smallest angle. Plugging in the other answer choices yields that only option <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> with a big angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/b/5/0b51850ccd3440214d4ccfbc14d29b2a31bfda55.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$140$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> and a small angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/5/9/e59e2c6e83eb78cca610a5fd4070ae01c8d4ae60.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> keeps the adjacent triangle’s interior angles sum equal to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/c/b/5cb1b41850ab676e4d1cf15c69b1609af4befbc5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$180$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" />. So the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/7/797c1d2e5cb34832c0ae44c52d209acf013bc847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" />.\n(Draw a diagram and draw supplementary angles to figure out the sum of the adjacent triangle’s interior angles.)\n</p><p>-Amon26\n</p><p>-chelsea_yan (a little bit of LATEX editing)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>It is obvious that of the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/1/601a7806cbfad68196c43a4665871f8c3186802e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> angles inside the convex hexagon, there are only two different angle measures: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of one and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/d/7cde695f2e4542fd01f860a89189f47a27143b66.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> of another. A convex quadrilateral formed by the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> trisector rays of any angle in the equilateral triangle and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> sides of the convex hexagon (such as the quadrilateral <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/6/be647e0859f234e42e038193d4f03083de378ada.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$BP_2P_1P_6$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"74\" height=\"17\" /> in the diagram above) will have an angle sum of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/b/96bd59024f5cb49b070ca9b0f8906499940c75e7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$360^{\\circ}$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, we have: <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/b/d/5bd8f96af614ccc30d0a151e6ee7ea4c4e007bd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$3a+3b=720 \\implies a+b=240$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"252\" height=\"16\" /> (total sum of all angles in a convex hexagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/6/c/d6c4dff656e402401294cffac1b472b740381ef4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$720^{\\circ}$\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />) and also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/5/f857f0256aee9d7abddca51ebe7222da95bba9c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20+2a+b=360 \\implies 2a+b=340$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"292\" height=\"16\" /> (the rays will form an inner angle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/6/8168f4b8585703cd5e6a8f85bd83540a7c5bfa74.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{60}{3}=20^{\\circ}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"74\" height=\"38\" />). Subtracting the two equations yields <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d9f33921a0d47f1bd9e67d8ea7cd1db87413ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a=100$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"60\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/8/f/78f1c62ecf36e305bbd65abe350390880d8bd8cb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b=140$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"61\" height=\"14\" />. Hence our smallest angle in this convex hexagon has degree measure <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/7/797c1d2e5cb34832c0ae44c52d209acf013bc847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" />. ~hxve\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_3_.28cheese.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3_(cheese)\">Solution 3 (cheese)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3 (cheese)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Notice that only answer choices <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/b/64b2f6f4ce90e90850b14e6b2a1c58c48a7d7258.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"18\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/6/f4664354cb7a37551bc83a2d6166b817162b0434.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" /> sum to 180, a familiar number, and since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/4/b/64b2f6f4ce90e90850b14e6b2a1c58c48a7d7258.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(a)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"22\" height=\"18\" /> is not a common answer, choose <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/4/6/f4664354cb7a37551bc83a2d6166b817162b0434.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$(c)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"20\" height=\"18\" />\nNote: this is a super informal (and risky) way to do this, use only if you can't draw a picture or have no idea.\n17:51, 6 November 2025 (EST)~Pungent_Muskrat\n</p><p><br />\nNote you can just draw the diagram (rulers and protractors are allowed!) and measure the angle, which is MUCH less risky. ~GoldenGem4848\n</p>\n<ul><li>protractors are not allowed on the AMC10! You are allowed a ruler and compass</li></ul>\n<p>While all of this is true it is more recommended to calculate it with a diagram to take less time.\n~Washingtonsee\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_4_.5BSIMPLE:_ONLY_ISOSCELES_TRIANGLES.5D\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4_[SIMPLE:_ONLY_ISOSCELES_TRIANGLES]\">Solution 4 [SIMPLE: ONLY ISOSCELES TRIANGLES]</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4 [SIMPLE: ONLY ISOSCELES TRIANGLES]\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://imgur.com/a/Hm7Bybf\">https://imgur.com/a/Hm7Bybf</a>\n</p><p>Angle A is split into three so the triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/e/c/9ece55f9a0c8c69d74514764c04b2a52b1cf734e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AEB$\" width=\"42\" height=\"13\" /> is an isosceles triangle \nbecause the bottom angles A and B are congruent and both <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/9/9498d610c387e904044dd016ade3bc445cb5d100.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Therefore, angle E is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/8/4886119534bab6d6981008596733884b1a9e42c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$140^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />, and the vertical angle in the hexagon \nis also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/8/8/4886119534bab6d6981008596733884b1a9e42c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$140^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Now find G. Triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/f/b/dfb86e5a3d5c63ac8571a333ef373d1a3f01f9e5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$CJD$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"44\" height=\"14\" /> is isosceles with angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> being <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/e/c/dec5a68a3382ed121445e64488834a165dbe0bd1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> \nbecause angle J in that triangle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/9/9498d610c387e904044dd016ade3bc445cb5d100.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Now angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> are known and sum to \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/4/6/546a1219877b552fbc107a7b2fa588bd4d9641ca.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$80 + 80 + 140 = 300$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"161\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>The pentagon <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/b/9/2b94c887be7deedfc124983500c1bf9c01a20e99.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DCFE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"60\" height=\"14\" /> and its other vertex (not named in my image) \nsum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/5/3/a531e51ec4324ea75e3f9a2fb306df9b74404abf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$540^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />.\n</p><p>So subtracting angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3355896da590fc491a10150a50416687626d7cc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ffb448918db29f2a72f8f87f421b3b3cad18f95.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/a/2/fa2fa899f0afb05d6837885523503a2d4df434f9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" />, and knowing that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/0/5/a055f405829e64a3b70253ab67cb45ed6ed5bb29.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$F$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> (let it be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />) \nis congruent (due to symmetry) to the other vertex angle (not named in my image),  \nwe have:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/c/7/0c7762831c298085cf45f1d07a6ab107b004fc2a.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[x + x = 240 \\implies x = 120^\\circ.\\]\" width=\"216\" height=\"14\" />\n</p><p>Thus, angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/2/6e28ce12d49d39f160d5a0ef54077fc98e4b9d2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$G$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"16\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b19d9486c9b7645405202c0869ec43682a65bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> because of triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/3/8/63879477e71d3e4ed6fa6716a2a655843e4fb032.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$FGE$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"45\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>Now find H. In isosceles triangle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/c/e/1cefdaae67a880bdfe08df38eede755722ca6bb4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AHB$\" width=\"44\" height=\"13\" />, \nangles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/1/9/019e9892786e493964e145e7c5cf7b700314e53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"15\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/f/5/ff5fb3d775862e2123b007eb4373ff6cc1a34d4e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"17\" height=\"14\" /> are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3388936ca5d53b685a625b9baac877382e278a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" />, so angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/1/9/b1902d279ba37d60bdce4e0e987b7cd19d48974e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$H$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"14\" /> is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b19d9486c9b7645405202c0869ec43682a65bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>Now find I. The red hexagon’s interior angles sum to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/4/4/a44e1fad45785327a361eab4a9f15b2f2304e9e4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$720^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"37\" height=\"15\" />, \nand angle <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is congruent to the angle across from it by symmetry.  \n</p><p>Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/2/7/027f4a11d6090f9eac0ce2488df6384dad1263ea.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$I$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> and its symmetric angle be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" />.  \nThen:\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/9/8/c987475ce7e77fcc9c1e2e34de557ed72cea412d.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[2x + 140(E) + 2(100)(G\\ \\&amp;\\ \\text{its symmetry}) + 100(H) = 720\\]\" width=\"449\" height=\"18\" />\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/5/2659005564d8f37d5b78f0380aeedd1ee9a026b3.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\implies x = 140^\\circ.\\]\" width=\"111\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>The smallest angle is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b19d9486c9b7645405202c0869ec43682a65bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" />.\n</p><p>~PUER_137\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_5_.5BMost_outer_triangle.5D\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5_[Most_outer_triangle]\">Solution 5 [Most outer triangle]</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5 [Most outer triangle]\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Using the outside triangle made by a trisection, we know that two of the angles are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/4/9/9498d610c387e904044dd016ade3bc445cb5d100.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^\\circ$\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/e/2/be2db659f0fe563687c7e6d278d950b5e0ceed63.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$60^\\circ,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"30\" height=\"16\" /> it follows that the third angle in the triangle, the foot of a trisection is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/7/b57192809d05742ff8ada422ffa193075dafd1ec.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100^\\circ.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"38\" height=\"13\" />\n</p><p>We then take a different triangle, that utilizes two of the same lines as the first triangle we examined and also has the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b19d9486c9b7645405202c0869ec43682a65bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> angle. This time, we can use the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3388936ca5d53b685a625b9baac877382e278a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> angle made by two of the trisections, and we get a triangle with angles <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/9/d/e9d2337ce30553feef943cf14ee1fb3a463d1bed.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40^\\circ, 40^\\circ, 100^\\circ.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"110\" height=\"18\" /> \n</p><p>We can look at a dart-like figure (inverted kite) and we get by symmetry, the angle opposite of the initial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/3/3/c3388936ca5d53b685a625b9baac877382e278a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"24\" height=\"13\" /> angle is also <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/0/8/2085613e67d48b3c954494517b6ba738627626f4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$40^\\circ,$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"33\" height=\"18\" /> there is also the middle angle formed by the trisection, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/5/835d8e848c33098aec0f318c790696e741b23147.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$20^\\circ.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"33\" height=\"15\" /> Using the dart theorem (I don't know why this isn't a thing when I search it up) we find that one angle in the hexagon is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/6/b/76b19d9486c9b7645405202c0869ec43682a65bd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$100^\\circ$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"33\" height=\"13\" /> and by symmetry, that is the smallest angle, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/7/797c1d2e5cb34832c0ae44c52d209acf013bc847.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{\\textbf{(C) }100}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -11px\" width=\"75\" height=\"30\" />\n</p><p>~happyfish0922\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_.28Logical.2C_Good_Editing.2C_.7E1:00.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_FormulaFocus_(Logical,_Good_Editing,_~1:00)\">Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:00)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by FormulaFocus (Logical, Good Editing, ~1:00)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/shorts/6on8yTokZBk\">https://www.youtube.com/shorts/6on8yTokZBk</a>\n</p><p>~Ebai\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_Daily_Dose_of_Math\">Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution by Daily Dose of Math\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://youtu.be/gPh9w3X3QSw\">https://youtu.be/gPh9w3X3QSw</a>\n</p><p>~Thesmartgreekmathdude\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Video_Solution.28Quick_and_Easy.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution(Quick_and_Easy)\">Video Solution(Quick and Easy)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10A_Problems/Problem_6&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: Video Solution(Quick and Easy)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p><a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external free\" href=\"https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ix1XPjyfOTE\">https://www.youtube.com/watch?v=ix1XPjyfOTE</a>\n~Sigma_Bob\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Video_Solution_by_DDM\">Video Solution by DDM</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2025_AMC_10